113|  //       Clean today. ⛔ AND DEAD ON ARRIVAL: the fixed query is `LIMIT ${IMPACT_FETCH_CAP + 1}`,
114|  //       so the literal "LIMIT 100" appears NOWHERE in the code and never will. Drop the `+ 1` —
115|  //       exactly the regression this test names — and the source still contains no "LIMIT 100".
116|  //       A prohibition on a spelling the codebase cannot write is a gate that cannot fail.
117|  //
118|  // ⭐⭐⭐ AND CONVERTING v3 TO `expectAbsentWithLiveMatcher` DID NOT SAVE IT. That helper proves
119|  // the MATCHER can fire against a canary. It says nothing about whether the SUBJECT could ever
120|  // reach a state the matcher would reject. Canaries bolted to a dead prohibition make it look
121|  // MORE rigorous while certifying exactly as much as before: nothing. The missing control is a
122|  // third one — HAS THIS SUBJECT EVER BEEN IN A STATE THIS ASSERTION WOULD REJECT? For a
123|  // prohibition added in the same commit that removed the thing it forbids, the answer is no and
124|  // can never become yes. (Found by an outside reviewer briefed to falsify, 2026-09-07.)
125|  //
126|  // ⇒ v4 ASSERTS THE BEHAVIOUR INSTEAD OF A SPELLING OF THE CODE. Feed the verb one more row than
127|  // it keeps and require it to notice. This is the assertion that goes red when the `+ 1` dies:
128|  // with `LIMIT CAP` the query returns exactly CAP rows, `edges.length > CAP` is false, and the
129|  // verb reports a saturated result as a plain count — the original defect, restored.
130|  it('★★★ THE REGRESSION: fed one more row than it keeps, graph_impact reports a FLOOR', async () => {
131|    repoRoot = await fanInRepo(CAP + 1);
132|    const out = await graphImpact({ repoRoot, symbol: 'target', depth: 1 });
133|    expect(out, 'a saturated result stated as a count is the defect the A/B found')
134|      .toMatch(/at least/i);
