138|  //
139|  // v4 went red against the fixed code, and the red was RIGHT. Driving the real verb on a fan-in of
140|  // 101 printed thirty rows and `TRUNCATED 70 more` — implying exactly 100 — and NO confidence line
141|  // at all. The floor language shipped that morning sits behind `if (suspicious)` in impact.js, and
142|  // a six-character symbol with one indexed node is not suspicious. ⇒ THE FIX HAD GONE INTO THE
143|  // LINE THAT SOMETIMES PRINTS AND NOT THE LINE THAT ALWAYS DOES, and `graph_callers` shared the
144|  // hole through `renderCompact`. Repaired once, in the renderer, and wired from both verbs.
145|  //
146|  // ⭐ A test that only ever agreed with the code would not have found that. This one disagreed.
147|  it('★★★ THE DISCRIMINATING CONTROL: display truncation WITHOUT saturation stays a plain count', async () => {
148|    // ⛔ THE 5-CALLER CASE IS TOO WEAK TO BE THE CONTROL. It emits no TRUNCATED line at all, so it
149|    // cannot tell "the floor fires only on saturation" from "the floor never fires". 40 callers
